@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ Two things start a Run without the user typing:
 - **A task timer** (`timer_create` with kind `task`). `superchat-timer-fire` appends `timer.fired` to the Workspace's most recently active conversation, tells the user, and then starts the task there: `timer.sending` is appended, the timer's text is sent, and `timer.started` names the Run that began. While an execution is running in that conversation nothing is sent and the task waits.
 - **A record's wait for a time.** `superchat-work-wake` opens a new conversation bound to the record's Workspace, appends `work.woken` (naming the Workspace's main conversation), clears the wait, makes the record that conversation's current record and sends the wake message. When sending does not finish, the wait is put back. When the first Run of that conversation ends, one line is left in the main conversation.
 
-Both go through the chat View's own send (`superchat-send-input` with `superchat-automated-input` bound), so Runtime remains the only thing that accepts the Run. Both write their "fired" fact **before** they send. That order means one occurrence never starts twice, including after a crash. What follows a send that does not happen differs between the two:
+Both go through the chat View's own send (`superchat-send-input` with `superchat-automated-input` bound), so Runtime remains the only thing that accepts the Run. Both write their "fired" fact **before** they send, so an occurrence is counted as come due once. Whether its execution started is a separate question, answered from later facts (`timer.sending`, `timer.started`, `timer.unconfirmed`; `work.woken` and the conversation's first `run.started`). When Emacs exited between the send and those facts and an execution began in that conversation afterwards, Superchat cannot tell whether the task ran: it says so and does not start it again. This is not a guarantee that an effect outside Emacs happens exactly once. What follows a send that does not happen differs between the two:
 
 - **Work Record** (`scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el`, 14 tests). A wake counts only when a Run started in its conversation: `superchat-work--due-p` reads `run.started` on that conversation's Tape. When the send signals, the wait is put back at once and the wake is tried again after `superchat-work-wake-retry-seconds` (300), at most `superchat-work-wake-retry-limit` (3) times in one Emacs. When Emacs exited between the facts and the send, the next scan (`superchat-work-wake-due`, run when the `work-records` row mounts) sees a wake whose conversation never ran and to whose record nothing was written since, and puts the wait back; the record is then taken up in a further new conversation. A record written to after the cut-off wake is left alone. One record's wake failing does not stop the scan.
 - **Timer** (`scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 12 tests). A task counts as started only when `timer.started` is on the Tape. A fired task without it (`superchat-timer-unstarted-tasks`) is started later by `superchat-timer-retry-tasks`: after `superchat-timer-retry-seconds` (300) when the send signalled, at most `superchat-timer-retry-limit` (3) times in one Emacs; after `superchat-timer-busy-retry-seconds` (30), without limit, when the conversation was busy; and first thing in `superchat-timer-catch-up` after a restart. The occurrence keeps its single `timer.fired`. One case is not started again: `timer.sending` is on record, `timer.started` is not, and a Run started in that conversation afterwards. That Run may be the task, so `timer.unconfirmed` is appended and the conversation is told that Superchat cannot tell. A fired task that has not started can be cancelled. Only a `timer.fired` carrying `awaits-start` takes part, so occurrences fired before these facts existed are left alone. The retry counts are in memory.
@@ -99,7 +99,7 @@ A woken record's new conversation has no recorded permission level, so it runs u
 
 Four things hold where an agent and the user share one Emacs ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-07-working-beside-the-user.md); `scripts/beside-user-regression.el`, 13 tests):
 
-- **An execution nobody started shows no review screen.** `superchat-changes--unattended-run-p` reads the conversation's Tape: a `timer.started` naming the Run, or a `work.woken` fact with the Run being that conversation's first. Such a Run's unsaved changes are announced in the echo area only; a Run the user started still gets the review screen, which takes the cursor.
+- **An execution nobody started shows no review screen.** `superchat-changes--unattended-run-p` reads the conversation's Tape: a `timer.started` naming the Run, or a `work.woken` fact with the Run being that conversation's first. Such a Run's unsaved changes are announced in the echo area, and its ending -- completed, failed or cancelled, with or without changes -- leaves a notice counted on the mode line (`[Superchat: N]`, `superchat-changes-notices`) until the user looks at it; the notices live in this Emacs only. A Run the user started still gets the review screen, which takes the cursor.
 - **A Run does not need its View.** Killing the conversation's buffer leaves the Run going; only an `ask_user` call made after that fails, with a result that tells the model the user cannot be asked. Approval questions do not depend on the View.
 - **A file that changed on disk is noticed before a structured edit** (`superchat-tools--disk-change-before-edit`, by `verify-visited-file-modtime`). A buffer without unsaved changes is reloaded and the edit is returned to the model to redo against the new text; a buffer with unsaved changes is edited and the result says that saving would overwrite the disk. `read_buffer` notes the difference. The user gets one echo-area line. Edits made through `eval_elisp` or a shell do not pass this check.
 - **A Work Record shown to the user names its executions** (`superchat-work-executions-text`): Run, conversation and outcome, oldest first. The record text a request carries is unchanged.
